<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qazi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qazi is the form of the Arabic word qadi, meaning a judge in Islamic law, used in Persian and South Asian contexts. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> qazi courts administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> as the formal legal framework of the state. The office continued under later Muslim rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta served as a <b>qazi</b>, or judge, at the court of Muhammad bin Tughlaq in Delhi from 1334.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The ulama, scholars of Islamic law, sat as <b>qazis</b>, from the district judge to the chief qazi who travelled with the imperial camp, and Sufi shaikhs kept shrines that emperors themselves visited: Akbar prayed at the tomb of Moinuddin Chishti at Ajmer and built Fatehpur Sikri beside the…</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>As imams and judges the ulama applied the sharia, and <b>qazis</b> judged cases in the provinces, ranking under the governors.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
